[219] (w=67)  Herdr uses a [1mhybrid agent-awareness model[22m:                        [0m]8;;
[220] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[221] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mProcess detection[22m figures out [1mwhat agent is in a pane[22m and       [0m]8;;
[222] (w=67)  whether it’s still alive.                                         [0m]8;;
[223] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mScreen heuristics[22m inspect the pane’s terminal output to infer   [0m]8;;
[224] (w=67)  state like:                                                       [0m]8;;
[225] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mworking[22m                                                     [0m]8;;
[226] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mblocked[22m                                                     [0m]8;;
[227] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1midle[22m                                                        [0m]8;;
[228] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mIntegrations/hooks[22m can report [1msemantic state directly[22m over      [0m]8;;
[229] (w=67)  Herdr’s local socket API when the agent supports it.              [0m]8;;
[230] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[231] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mIn practice[22m[39m                                                   [0m]8;;
[232] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[233] (w=67)  Out of the box, Herdr works with [1mzero config[22m by combining:        [0m]8;;
[234] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[235] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mforeground process detection[22m                                    [0m]8;;
[236] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mterminal output heuristics[22m                                      [0m]8;;
[237] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[238] (w=67)  So it can look at things like:                                    [0m]8;;
[239] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mthe foreground process name                                     [0m]8;;
[240] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mvisible terminal text/spinners/prompts                          [0m]8;;
[241] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mapproval/confirmation UI patterns                               [0m]8;;
[242] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[243] (w=67)  That’s how it knows a pane is likely:                             [0m]8;;
[244] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mblocked[22m: waiting for approval/input                             [0m]8;;
[245] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mworking[22m: actively processing                                    [0m]8;;
[246] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1midle[22m: finished / not actively running                           [0m]8;;
[247] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[248] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mWhen hooks are available[22m[39m                                      [0m]8;;
[249] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[250] (w=67)  For agents like [1mpi, claude code, codex, and opencode[22m, Herdr can   [0m]8;;
[251] (w=67)  install integrations that send state directly to it over the      [0m]8;;
[252] (w=67)  socket API.                                                       [0m]8;;
[253] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[254] (w=67)  That makes status reporting more reliable because the agent says  [0m]8;;
[255] (w=67)  things like:                                                      [0m]8;;
[256] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m“I’m working”                                                   [0m]8;;
[257] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m“I’m blocked”                                                   [0m]8;;
[258] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m“I’m idle”                                                      [0m]8;;
[259] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[260] (w=67)  Herdr’s docs describe this as:                                    [0m]8;;
[261] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[262] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mprocess detection owns pane identity and liveness[22m               [0m]8;;
[263] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mintegrations enrich semantic state[22m                              [0m]8;;
[264] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mscreen heuristics remain the fallback[22m                           [0m]8;;
[265] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[266] (w=67)  So hooks [1mdo not replace[22m process detection. They just              [0m]8;;
[267] (w=67)  override/improve the displayed state while active.                [0m]8;;
[268] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[269] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1m“Done” vs “Idle”[22m[39m                                              [0m]8;;
[270] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[271] (w=67)  Herdr also tracks whether you’ve [1mseen[22m a pane since it became      [0m]8;;
[272] (w=67)  idle:                                                             [0m]8;;
[273] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[274] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mdone[22m = finished, but you haven’t looked at it yet               [0m]8;;
[275] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1midle[22m = finished and already seen                                [0m]8;;
[276] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[277] (w=67)  That’s how it can highlight panes/workspaces that need your       [0m]8;;
[278] (w=67)  attention.                                                        [0m]8;;
[279] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[280] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mWorkspace-level awareness[22m[39m                                     [0m]8;;
[281] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[282] (w=67)  Pane states roll up into workspace state with priority roughly    [0m]8;;
[283] (w=67)  like:                                                             [0m]8;;
[284] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[285] (w=67)  [1mblocked > done/unseen > working > idle[22m                            [0m]8;;
[286] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[287] (w=67)  So the sidebar can show the most urgent workspace at a glance.    [0m]8;;
[288] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[289] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mShort version[22m[39m                                                 [0m]8;;
[290] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[291] (w=67)  Herdr’s agent awareness is:                                       [0m]8;;
[292] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[293] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m1. [39m[1mdetect the agent process[22m                                       [0m]8;;
[294] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m2. [39m[1mread the terminal screen[22m                                       [0m]8;;
[295] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m3. [39m[1maccept direct state reports from hooks/plugins when available[22m  [0m]8;;
[296] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m4. [39m[1mroll pane state up to tabs/workspaces[22m                          [0m]8;;
[297] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m5. [39m[1mdistinguish unseen-finished panes as “done”[22m                    [0m]8;;
[298] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[299] (w=67)  If you want, I can also show you [1mhow Herdr detects Pi             [0m]8;;
[300] (w=67) ]133;B]133;C [1mspecifically[22m or [1mwhich events each integration sends[22m.              [0m]8;;
[301] (w=0) [0m]8;;
