[319] (w=67)  Yes — [1mmostly[22m. You can get surprisingly close in tmux, especially  [0m]8;;
[320] (w=67)  for:                                                              [0m]8;;
[321] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[322] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mtagging panes/windows that run agents[22m                           [0m]8;;
[323] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mtracking a per-pane agent state[22m like [38;2;158;206;106mworking[39m / [38;2;158;206;106mblocked[39m / [38;2;158;206;106midle[39m   [0m]8;;
[324] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1msearching/jumping to agent panes[22m                                [0m]8;;
[325] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mbuilding a popup-based “agent sessionizer”[22m                      [0m]8;;
[326] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[327] (w=67)  What’s harder to replicate cleanly is Herdr’s full experience:    [0m]8;;
[328] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[329] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mautomatic, robust [1mscreen-based agent awareness[22m                  [0m]8;;
[330] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39ma native [1msidebar / workspace rollup[22m                             [0m]8;;
[331] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mconsistent [1mdone vs idle[22m attention tracking                      [0m]8;;
[332] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39ma built-in control API centered around agents                   [0m]8;;
[333] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[334] (w=67)  But for your use case — “detect windows with agents and search    [0m]8;;
[335] (w=67)  for them” — [1mtmux is absolutely enough[22m.                            [0m]8;;
[336] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[337] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1mBest approach in tmux[22m[39m                                             [0m]8;;
[338] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[339] (w=67)  Think of it as 3 layers:                                          [0m]8;;
[340] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[341] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1m1. Identity: “is this pane an agent?”[22m[39m                         [0m]8;;
[342] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[343] (w=67)  Use tmux pane metadata to decide whether a pane is running:       [0m]8;;
[344] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[345] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mpi[39m                                                              [0m]8;;
[346] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mclaude[39m                                                          [0m]8;;
[347] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mcodex[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[348] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mopencode[39m                                                        [0m]8;;
[349] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39metc.                                                            [0m]8;;
[350] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[351] (w=67)  You can detect this from:                                         [0m]8;;
[352] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[353] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m#{pane_current_command}[39m                                         [0m]8;;
[354] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m#{pane_start_command}[39m                                           [0m]8;;
[355] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mwrapper scripts / aliases                                       [0m]8;;
[356] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mexplicit tmux user options like [38;2;158;206;106m@agent[39m and [38;2;158;206;106m@agent_state[39m         [0m]8;;
[357] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[358] (w=67)  The cleanest path is [1mnot[22m guessing from raw shell commands if you  [0m]8;;
[359] (w=67)  can avoid it.                                                     [0m]8;;
[360] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[361] (w=67)  Instead, launch agents through wrappers that mark the pane.       [0m]8;;
[362] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[363] (w=67)  Example idea:                                                     [0m]8;;
[364] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[365] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```bash[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[366] (w=67)    [38;2;86;95;137m# ~/bin/tmux-agent-run[39m                                          [0m]8;;
[367] (w=67)    agent=[38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$1[39m"[39m; [38;2;125;207;255mshift[39m                                               [0m]8;;
[368] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[369] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$agent[39m"[39m              [0m]8;;
[370] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent_state [38;2;158;206;106m"working"[39m       [0m]8;;
[371] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[372] (w=67)    [38;2;125;207;255mexec[39m [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$agent[39m"[39m [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$@[39m"[39m                                              [0m]8;;
[373] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[374] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[375] (w=67)  Then aliases like:                                                [0m]8;;
[376] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[377] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```bash[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[378] (w=67)    [38;2;125;207;255malias[39m pi=[38;2;158;206;106m'tmux-agent-run pi'[39m                                    [0m]8;;
[379] (w=67)    [38;2;125;207;255malias[39m claude=[38;2;158;206;106m'tmux-agent-run claude'[39m                            [0m]8;;
[380] (w=67)    [38;2;125;207;255malias[39m codex=[38;2;158;206;106m'tmux-agent-run codex'[39m                              [0m]8;;
[381] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[382] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[383] (w=67)  That gives you a reliable per-pane identity.                      [0m]8;;
[384] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[385] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m [0m]8;;
[386] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[387] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1m2. State: “what is the agent doing?”[22m[39m                          [0m]8;;
[388] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[389] (w=67)  This is the part Herdr does with a hybrid model. In tmux, you can [0m]8;;
[390] (w=67)  do something similar:                                             [0m]8;;
[391] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[392] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m#### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mOption A: Hook/plugin integration[22m[39m                            [0m]8;;
[393] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[394] (w=67)  Best option.                                                      [0m]8;;
[395] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[396] (w=67)  If the agent has hooks/events, have those call tmux:              [0m]8;;
[397] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[398] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```bash[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[399] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent_state blocked         [0m]8;;
[400] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent_state working         [0m]8;;
[401] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent_state idle            [0m]8;;
[402] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[403] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[404] (w=67)  That’s much more reliable than scraping text.                     [0m]8;;
[405] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[406] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m#### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mOption B: Heuristics via [22m[39m[38;2;158;206;106mcapture-pane[39m                        [0m]8;;
[407] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[408] (w=67)  Fallback option.                                                  [0m]8;;
[409] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[410] (w=67)  You can poll pane contents:                                       [0m]8;;
[411] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[412] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```bash[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[413] (w=67)    tmux capture-pane -p -t %12 -S -200                             [0m]8;;
[414] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[415] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[416] (w=67)  Then parse for patterns like:                                     [0m]8;;
[417] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[418] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mWorking...[39m                                                      [0m]8;;
[419] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mPress ESC to interrupt[39m                                          [0m]8;;
[420] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mAllow? [y/n][39m                                                    [0m]8;;
[421] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mPermissionRequest[39m                                               [0m]8;;
[422] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mspinner lines                                                   [0m]8;;
[423] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mapprove?[39m                                                        [0m]8;;
[424] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[425] (w=67)  This can work, but it’s inherently brittle.                       [0m]8;;
[426] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[427] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m#### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mOption C: Hybrid[22m[39m                                             [0m]8;;
[428] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[429] (w=67)  Best overall tmux strategy:                                       [0m]8;;
[430] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[431] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mhooks/plugins when available                                    [0m]8;;
[432] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mpane text heuristics otherwise                                  [0m]8;;
[433] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mprocess name only for identity/liveness                         [0m]8;;
[434] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[435] (w=67)  That is basically the same conceptual model Herdr uses.           [0m]8;;
[436] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[437] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m [0m]8;;
[438] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[439] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1m3. Search/jump UI[22m[39m                                             [0m]8;;
[440] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[441] (w=67)  This is easy in tmux and fits your popup/sessionizer flow well.   [0m]8;;
[442] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[443] (w=67)  Build a script that lists panes across all sessions/windows:      [0m]8;;
[444] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[445] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```bash[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[446] (w=67)    tmux list-panes -a -F                                           [0m]8;;
[447] (w=67)  [38;2;158;206;106m'#{session_name}:#{window_index}.#{pane_index} #{pane_id}         [0m]8;;
[448] (w=67)  [38;2;158;206;106m#{pane_current_command} #{@agent} #{@agent_state} #{pane_title}'[39m  [0m]8;;
[449] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[450] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[451] (w=67)  Then feed that into [38;2;158;206;106mfzf[39m inside a popup:                           [0m]8;;
[452] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[453] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```tmux[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[454] (w=67)    [39mbind-key C-a display-popup -E                                   [0m]8;;
[455] (w=67)  "~/.config/tmux/scripts/agent-search"[39m                             [0m]8;;
[456] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[457] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[458] (w=67)  Where [38;2;158;206;106magent-search[39m does roughly:                                  [0m]8;;
[459] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[460] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m1. [39mcollect all panes                                              [0m]8;;
[461] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m2. [39mfilter to panes with [38;2;158;206;106m@agent[39m set, or infer from command         [0m]8;;
[462] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m3. [39mshow session/window/pane + agent + state                       [0m]8;;
[463] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m4. [39mon selection:                                                  [0m]8;;
[464] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mswitch-client -t session[39m                                    [0m]8;;
[465] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mselect-window -t session:window[39m                             [0m]8;;
[466] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mselect-pane -t pane[39m                                         [0m]8;;
[467] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[468] (w=67)  That gives you searchable “find all agents” behavior.             [0m]8;;
[469] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[470] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m [0m]8;;
[471] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[472] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1mA practical tmux design[22m[39m                                           [0m]8;;
[473] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[474] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mPer-pane metadata[22m[39m                                             [0m]8;;
[475] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[476] (w=67)  Use tmux user options:                                            [0m]8;;
[477] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[478] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m@agent[39m → [38;2;158;206;106mpi[39m, [38;2;158;206;106mclaude[39m, [38;2;158;206;106mcodex[39m                                      [0m]8;;
[479] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m@agent_state[39m → [38;2;158;206;106mworking[39m, [38;2;158;206;106mblocked[39m, [38;2;158;206;106midle[39m, [38;2;158;206;106mdone[39m, [38;2;158;206;106munknown[39m            [0m]8;;
[480] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m@agent_seen[39m → [38;2;158;206;106m0[39m / [38;2;158;206;106m1[39m                                             [0m]8;;
[481] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mmaybe [38;2;158;206;106m@agent_label[39m → custom name                                [0m]8;;
[482] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[483] (w=67)  tmux lets you set pane-local options with [38;2;158;206;106m-p[39m.                     [0m]8;;
[484] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[485] (w=67)  Example:                                                          [0m]8;;
[486] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[487] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```bash[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[488] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent pi                    [0m]8;;
[489] (w=67)    tmux set-option -p -t [38;2;158;206;106m"[38;2;255;158;100m$TMUX_PANE[39m"[39m @agent_state working         [0m]8;;
[490] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[491] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[492] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m [0m]8;;
[493] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[494] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mAgent search popup[22m[39m                                            [0m]8;;
[495] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[496] (w=67)  Add a key:                                                        [0m]8;;
[497] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[498] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```tmux[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[499] (w=67)    [39mbind-key C-g display-popup -E                                   [0m]8;;
[500] (w=67)  "~/.config/tmux/scripts/agent-search"[39m                             [0m]8;;
[501] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[502] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[503] (w=67)  Search rows might look like:                                      [0m]8;;
[504] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[505] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```text[39m                                                           [0m]8;;
[506] (w=67)    work:1.0  %3  pi      working  api                              [0m]8;;
[507] (w=67)    work:2.1  %8  claude  blocked  docs                             [0m]8;;
[508] (w=67)    ops:0.0   %14 codex   idle     deploy                           [0m]8;;
[509] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m```[39m                                                               [0m]8;;
[510] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[511] (w=67)  Then jump to the selected pane.                                   [0m]8;;
[512] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[513] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m [0m]8;;
[514] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[515] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m### [22m[39m[38;2;255;158;100m[1mWindow/session tagging[22m[39m                                        [0m]8;;
[516] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[517] (w=67)  If any pane in a window is blocked, mark the window:              [0m]8;;
[518] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[519] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mset [38;2;158;206;106m@window_has_agent 1[39m                                         [0m]8;;
[520] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mset [38;2;158;206;106m@window_agent_state blocked[39m                                 [0m]8;;
[521] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[522] (w=67)  You can compute this from pane metadata and maybe decorate window [0m]8;;
[523] (w=67)  names/status.                                                     [0m]8;;
[524] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[525] (w=67)  Example ideas for status-right or window-status-format:           [0m]8;;
[526] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[527] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m●[39m red = blocked                                                 [0m]8;;
[528] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m●[39m yellow = working                                              [0m]8;;
[529] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m●[39m blue = done                                                   [0m]8;;
[530] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m●[39m green = idle                                                  [0m]8;;
[531] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[532] (w=67)  That gives you mini-Herdr vibes inside tmux.                      [0m]8;;
[533] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[534] (w=67)  [38;2;59;66;97m─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────[39m [0m]8;;
[535] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[536] (w=67)  [38;2;255;158;100m[1m“Done” vs “Idle” in tmux[22m[39m                                          [0m]8;;
[537] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[538] (w=67)  This is possible, but a bit awkward.                              [0m]8;;
[539] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[540] (w=67)  Herdr distinguishes:                                              [0m]8;;
[541] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[542] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1mdone[22m = finished, unseen                                         [0m]8;;
[543] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[1midle[22m = finished, seen                                           [0m]8;;
[544] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[545] (w=67)  In tmux you could do:                                             [0m]8;;
[546] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[547] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mwhen state changes [38;2;158;206;106mworking -> idle[39m, set:                        [0m]8;;
[548] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m@agent_state idle[39m                                           [0m]8;;
[549] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m@agent_seen 0[39m                                               [0m]8;;
[550] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39mwhen user focuses that pane, set:                               [0m]8;;
[551] (w=67)      [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106m@agent_seen 1[39m                                               [0m]8;;
[552] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
[553] (w=67)  Then your UI can display:                                         [0m]8;;
[554] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106mdone[39m if [38;2;158;206;106m@agent_state=idle[39m and [38;2;158;206;106m@agent_seen=0[39m                     [0m]8;;
[555] (w=67)  [38;2;125;207;255m- [39m[38;2;158;206;106midle[39m if [38;2;158;206;106m@agent_state=idle[39m and [38;2;158;206;106m@agent_seen=1[39m                     [0m]8;;
[556] (w=67)                                                                    [0m]8;;
